  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe weather in our forecast area looks vastly different than it\ndid this time yesterday. Convective debris spilling in from\nstorms firing up in the Ohio Valley this afternoon has kept us\nin a more stable state, with temperatures hovering in the mid\n70s. Satellite views upstream sheds light on the path of least \nresistance of today's storms, currently taking a southeast dive\nfrom Indiana into Kentucky. The eastern flank of this line,\nencompassing much of Ohio, has retained mostly stratiform rain\nwith some embedded thunder, and hasn't made much forward\nprogress into the Ohio River Valley throughout the course of the\nday.\n\nHi-res model guidance tends to struggle with a weather pattern\nsuch as this, so closely monitoring satellite and radar trends\nwill be apparent for situational awareness and forecast updates\nheading into this evening and overnight. Any showers or storms \nthat reach the Central Appalachians through the near term are \nnot expected to reach severity levels such as what we faced \nyesterday, but could impose strong wind gusts and torrential \nrainfall. Given very saturated soils from previous rainfall this\nweek, any heavy downpour that reaches the forecast area could\nyield localized high water issues. No changes were made to the\nongoing Flood Watch, which remains intact through Friday morning\namid this active weather pattern. Convective trends for the area \nwill remain indicative of upstream evolution of storms each day\nthrough the end of this week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe upper level weather pattern shows no signs of varying\nthrough this week, maintaining the Central Appalachians in an \narea prone to daily showers and thunderstorms. The start of the \nweekend trends slightly drier with the mass exodus of the \nstalled front, but may quickly fill back in with additional \nconvection aiming for the Central Appalachians for Sunday and \nonward. Limited severe and hydro concerns are expected in the\nlong term outlook as it currently stands.",
